FreeRDP 3.33.1: decode H.264 with a V4L2 stateful decoder when ffmpeg
has one (h264_v4l2m2m), such as the Steam Frame's qcom-iris. FreeRDP's
hardware decoding goes through ffmpeg's hwaccel device types (VA-API,
Vulkan, ...), and a SoC's V4L2 decoder is none of those: ffmpeg wraps
it as a decoder of its own, which wants the stream's frame size before
it opens, hands a frame back a few milliseconds after its packet, and
returns NV12. The path runs beside the software decoder, which stays
open and takes over when the device is missing, busy, refuses the size
or stops answering. FREERDP_H264_V4L2M2M=0 turns it off.

On the Frame, 1080p at 60 fps through avc420_decompress: 4.7 ms of CPU
a frame against 7.4 in software, the rest being the conversion to RGB,
and the same pictures bit for bit.

